come on mermaid  in the heart of the welsh valleys farmer  mossy hassanin tends to his land and  animals he's one of a small group of  people from ethnic minorities in the uk  who are working in farming  an industry that's nearly 100  white  it's not really something that you know  people from ethnic backgrounds really  look at because  it's like it's a very very closed  group you know um you know even just  going to the auction house for example  you don't know what's going on you know  that was sitting there we went there one  time and i was like what is going on  here like this is another language i  never saw myself as a farmer i saw  myself as someone who bought land it  dropped a few years later when i was  like i've got cows i've got goats i've  got geese i've got ducks or chickens i'm  farming this is farming you know so  that's when i kind of call myself the  british muslim farmer at that point  figures show that last year 97.2  of the british agriculture forestry and  fishing sector was made up of white  workers excluding seasonal staff some of  the reasons are obvious rural parts of  the country are less diverse farms tend  to be handed down through the  generations but now one of the country's  leading farming institutions wants to  tackle the issue  the royal agricultural university in  gloucestershire is launching two new  undergraduate scholarships exclusively  for those from ethnic minority groups we  definitely think that there needs to be  space for everyone to to play a part in  in agriculture in the land-based sector  if we can educate more people if we can  train more people from a broader range  of backgrounds  then there'll be more people available  in the future who have got the skills  and the qualifications and the interest  to actually go on and make a difference  in that sector and in those jobs  [Applause]  at the stepney city farm in london young  people from all backgrounds get hands-on  experience  but many feel farming isn't an easy  industry to enter i think it's partly to  do with not knowing that farming is an  acceptable profession to go into and not  knowing how to go into it not knowing  that it  is a job because for a lot of people  it's just what everyone does in their  home country  food experts say diversity in our  population should be reflected in food  production we do want to break that mold  over being uh you know  ultimately an old white man and a  tractor what we do need to see is a  better diversity within the foods that  we're producing and those skills are  being lost if they're not being  invested into our entire food and  farming  sector while there aren't calls for  diversity quotas in farming many believe  more work is needed to ensure the  industry is open to all  that white is sky news in south wales

